  "comments": [],
  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Caesar locked fifteen hundred pounds of a plant in his treasury. Ancient Romans called it silphium, a golden-flowered stalk that cured illnesses, spiced royal feasts, and prevented pregnancy.",
        "visual_query": "ancient roman forum treasury ruins",
        "visual_fallback": "roman stone architecture"
      },
      {
        "narration": "It was worth its weight in silver, but it only grew along a narrow strip of North African coast. Whenever farmers tried cultivating it anywhere else, the seeds refused to grow.",
        "visual_query": "cyrene libya ancient mediterranean coastline",
        "visual_fallback": "mediterranean rocky coast"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Why did Rome\u2019s most valuable crop vanish without a trace? By the first century, Emperor Nero was reportedly presented with the final surviving stalk as a curiosity, and silphium disappeared from history for two thousand years.",
        "visual_query": "ancient roman bust emperor nero",
        "visual_fallback": "roman marble statue"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Until a botanist in Turkey noticed a rare mountain fennel that local villagers had protected for generations. It matched ancient Roman coin engravings, bled the same thick resin, and packed identical medicinal compounds.",
        "visual_query": "mount hasan cappadocia turkey landscape",
        "visual_fallback": "rocky mountain landscape"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Known today as Ferula drudeana, Rome\u2019s lost wonder herb never actually died out\u2014it had simply been hiding on a quiet hillside in plain sight.",
        "visual_query": "yellow flowering wild fennel plant",
        "visual_fallback": "yellow wildflowers blooming"
      }
    ],
